WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.934
-My next guest is currently
in his ninth season

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:06.167
of "Chicago Fire," which airs
Wednesday nights at 9:00 p.m.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:09.234
on NBC and is streaming
on Peacock right now.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:11.534
Here is Taylor Kinney!

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:12.801
Taylor!

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:13.934
-Hey!

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:16.000
-It is so nice to see you.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.801
And thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:21.467
I've actually met you before
but very briefly.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:26.501
-This was, I forget the year --
2016, maybe something like that.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:29.133
But every year -- I think
I've done it four times --

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:32.267
we do a Polar Plunge,
which is a charity event

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.200
for the Special Olympics
in Chicago.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.834
They wait for, you know,
in and around the coldest day

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:40.501
of the year and they push
some ice and debris

00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:41.901
up from Lake Michigan.

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:46.300
You get your swim trunks on,
and you jump in.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.634
And it's gotten bigger,
the crowds.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:50.067
But I remember you were there.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:53.167
Vince Vaughn was there, and I
remember you had a suit on.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:55.634
And you went, like,
just all full suit.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.067
And I go,
"How's he gonna get out?

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.601
That's gotta be, like,
triple as cold."

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.400
-Look, there it is right there.
-There you go.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.367
-I remember seeing you. My hair
was frozen. It was spiky.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:05.934
-Oh, yeah, everything
just goes --

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:07.567
-Ice.
-Yeah.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.634
And I'm sure your suit is
still probably stiff as a board.

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:13.000
-It's thawing out, yeah.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.434
It's halfway from Chicago
to New York. Yeah.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:18.234
But I love that you did that
four times.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:19.567
Good for you.

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:21.534
That's unbelievable.

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:24.734
It's not a surprise to me
that you do these things

00:01:24.734 --> 00:01:26.100
'cause you're in great shape.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.868
But also you like
a good challenge.

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:30.767
I know you and your brothers

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.601
have big grilling competitions
with each other.

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:34.934
-Yeah.
-How did that start?

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.767
-I kind of grew up on
fish sticks and meat loaf

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:40.367
and leftover fish sticks
and leftover chicken.

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:42.701
And so it was always --

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.968
I just expected things
to taste like that.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:46.534
And I was working a lot.

00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:49.167
I started kind of coming home
and grilling on my own,

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.434
like, towards the end of high
school, beginning of college.

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:55.167
I would go, if I was doing
carpentry work or roofing,

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.167
I started grilling, and I
started with these marinades.

00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:01.734
And then, it was like,
"Hey, Mom, like,

00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:05.868
you actually can make chicken
that doesn't crack and dry.

00:02:05.868 --> 00:02:08.200
Like, this is delicious.
It's kind of good."

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.434
And so it was a joke with that,
and then we got more into it,

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:13.300
started writing down recipes.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.334
My brothers got in on it.

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.467
And then it became
a competition of sorts

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:18.901
and it just kind of took off.

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:20.601
-Well, you took it
to the next level.

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:23.834
You actually bought
a restaurant.

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:27.133
-So, I own -- part-own
a restaurant and theater

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:28.868
in Lancaster, Pennsylvania.

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:32.067
It's called Zoetropolis
and Lancaster Distilleries.

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:33.634
The theater --
It's a small theater.

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:38.467
We do independent film
documentaries, live music.

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:43.200
It's not a big venue, maybe
90-person capacity pre-COVID.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.801
It's helping a lot of people

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:48.200
who are in between jobs
and in and out of work.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.167
-Please support
your local stuff. Yeah.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:54.334
-If you ever get a chance, like,
come and do a little stage --

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:57.968
a little set on the stage
there at Zoetropolis.

00:02:57.968 --> 00:02:59.801
-If you have an open mike,
I can't help myself.

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:01.300
I have to go up
and grab onto it.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.801
Zoetropolis?

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:04.767
-Zoetropolis is the theater.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.100
Lancaster Distilleries
is the restaurant.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.467
-Alright. Watch me.

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:09.868
I'm going to come.
I'm going to come for you.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.968
-[ Laughs ] I love it.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.934
-I want to say
congrats on "Chicago Fire."

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:16.634
It's a giant hit.

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:18.934
I mean, this is season 9 now?

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:20.200
-Yes.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.400
-I mean, what is it like
when you first get that job

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.100
and you go,
"Oh, this could be fun.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.801
Let's see if we can do this."

00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:29.801
Cut to -- what is it? --
10 years later now.

00:03:29.801 --> 00:03:31.734
You're still --
You're crushing it.

00:03:31.734 --> 00:03:34.133
It's still a major hit on NBC.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:37.100
-It was one of those things --
I had done pilots before.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.467
Either they go --
they don't get picked up.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:40.901
It's kind of --
You know, it's a crap shoot.

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.400
You don't know.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.734
But, you know, with Dick Wolf
being a part of this

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:45.801
and "Law and Order," I said,

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:48.167
"Well, this could have
some legs."

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:49.934
I think it was one of
our producers saying

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:52.200
or a showrunner said,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.200
"Hey, you might want to
look into getting a place.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.467
Not buying but just,
hey, you're going to --

00:03:57.467 --> 00:03:58.634
Get out of the hotel."

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:01.000
And it's been nine years now.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.367
-Wow!

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.133
-It's the closest thing as
a 9:00 to 5:00 that I think

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:07.334
that I'll experience
in this line of work.

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:09.767
-I'm a fan of the show,
and I have to ask,

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.801
because I used to live
by a fire station

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.734
when I was first
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:18.901
And they are
a very honest bunch.

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:21.734
Do you hear from
real firefighters who watch

00:04:21.734 --> 00:04:24.167
and go like, "We would never do
that. What are you doing?"

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:28.300
-For the most part,
it was positive responses.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.167
But I think in the first couple
episodes, they were like,

00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:34.234
"Well, you guys,
you got this hose.

00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:35.334
You slid down this hose.

00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:36.567
How the hell are you
gonna get back up?

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:39.033
We don't do that.
What is that?"

00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:41.501
-"It's a TV show!

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.834
It's a television show!
We're not really firefighters."

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:45.100
-That's what I said.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.400
I was like, "There's
a little creative license."

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.501
They're like,
"What are you talking about?

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:50.901
No, no."

00:04:50.901 --> 00:04:53.467
-Taylor, thanks so much
for coming on the show, man.

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:54.801
I'm a big fan.

00:04:54.801 --> 00:04:56.334
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:58.801
Here's Taylor Kinney
in "Chicago Fire."

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.434
Take a look at this.

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:02.000
-You alright?

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.901
-Just waiting for some aspirin
to kick in.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.901
What's up?

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:08.667
-Do you know anything
about the delivery

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:10.167
Stella ordered
for the apartment?

00:05:10.167 --> 00:05:11.734
-Delivery of what?

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:14.234
-That's the thing.
She wouldn't say.

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:15.868
I don't deal well with change.

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:17.601
She knows that.

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:19.834
-Well, it's her home, too.

00:05:19.834 --> 00:05:22.868
She probably wants to put
her stamp on the place.

00:05:22.868 --> 00:05:24.901
-Mm. Probably.

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:29.033
-Hey, uh, you and Kidd,
if it's time for me to move on

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:32.434
and you guys need the loft to
yourselves, just say the word.

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:34.467
I promise. I'm all good.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.601
-Didn't I just say I don't
deal well with change?

00:05:36.601 --> 00:05:37.901
-Taylor Kinney.

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:41.534
"Chicago Fire" airs Wednesdays
at 9:00 p.m. on NBC.

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:44.467
And every season is streaming
right now on Peacock.

